@using FairShare.Contracts.Calculation

@* The Alabama court-prep surface (#235), the CS-42 sibling of OregonCourtPrep: informational
   only, no deviation math. Alabama has no Oregon-style +/-15% agreed band - the presumption
   moves through Rule 32(A)'s written-agreement or written-findings paths instead - so this
   card explains those, the Rule 32(A)(1) deviation reasons, and the standardized filings.
   Copy follows the /terms discipline: literally true against Rule 32, Ala. R. Jud. Admin.
   The deviation list stays rendered (hidden) while collapsed so the toggle's aria-controls
   target exists whether the disclosure is open or closed. *@

<div class="card mt-3">
    <div class="card-header text-center"><strong>Preparing for court or mediation</strong></div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p class="mb-1">
            <strong>The recommended order is presumed correct.</strong>
            @if (Result is { FinalAmount: > 0 } r && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(r.Payer))
            {
                <text>
                    A court starts from the presumption that the worksheet amount above -
                    $@r.FinalAmount.ToString("N0") a month from the @r.Payer.ToLowerInvariant() -
                    is the right child-support award (Rule 32(A), Ala. R. Jud. Admin.).
                </text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>
                    A court starts from the presumption that the worksheet result above is the
                    right child-support award (Rule 32(A), Ala. R. Jud. Admin.).
                </text>
            }
        </p>
        <p class="mb-3">
            That presumption can be rebutted two ways: a <strong>fair, written agreement</strong>
            between the parents establishing a different amount and stating the reasons for it,
            or the court determining from the evidence that applying the guidelines would be
            <strong>manifestly unjust or inequitable</strong>. Either way, a deviation must be
            supported by the court's written findings - Alabama has no fixed percentage band for
            agreed amounts.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-1">
            <strong>The judge may ask about things the worksheet doesn't show.</strong>
            Rule 32(A)(1) lists reasons that may support a deviation - be ready to speak to any
            that apply to you:
        </p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm px-0"
                @onclick="() => reasonsExpanded = !reasonsExpanded"
                aria-expanded="@reasonsExpanded"
                aria-controls="AL_DeviationReasons">
            @(reasonsExpanded ? "Hide the deviation reasons" : "Show the deviation reasons")
        </button>
        <div id="AL_DeviationReasons" hidden="@(!reasonsExpanded)">
            <ul class="small mb-1">
                <li>Shared physical custody or visitation giving the paying parent substantially more time with the children than courts customarily order</li>
                <li>Extraordinary transportation costs for visitation, borne substantially by one parent</li>
                <li>College-education expenses incurred before a child reaches the age of majority (19 in Alabama)</li>
                <li>Assets of, or unearned income received by or for, the children</li>
                <li>The custodial parent will not be claiming the income-tax exemptions for the children (the guidelines assume they will)</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="small text-muted mb-0">
                The list is not exclusive, and none of these reasons requires the court to
                deviate - they may simply be considered.
            </p>
        </div>

        <p class="mb-1 mt-3">
            <strong>Income can be imputed.</strong>
            If a parent is voluntarily unemployed or underemployed, the court estimates what that
            parent could earn and calculates support from that imputed income (Rule 32(B)(5)).
            FairShare uses the incomes you enter - if imputation is on the table, run the numbers
            with the imputed figure too.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-3">
            <strong>Extraordinary expenses can be added.</strong>
            Beyond the recommended order, a court may make additional awards for extraordinary
            medical, dental, and educational expenses (Rule 32(C)(4)).
        </p>

        <p class="mb-1">
            <strong>Bring the standardized paperwork.</strong>
            Alabama's child-support filings use standardized forms:
        </p>
        <ul class="small mb-0">
            <li>Form <strong>CS-41</strong> - each parent's Child-Support-Obligation Income Statement/Affidavit</li>
            <li>Form <strong>CS-42</strong> (or <strong>CS-42-S</strong> for shared 50/50 custody) - the worksheet itself; the Export button fills the official workbook for the form you are viewing</li>
            <li>Form <strong>CS-43</strong> - the Child-Support-Guidelines Notice of Compliance</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="text-muted small mt-3 mb-0">
            This is preparation guidance, not a prediction - whether any reason changes the amount
            is the court's call. FairShare does not compute deviations.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public CalculationResponse? Result { get; set; }

    private bool reasonsExpanded;
}
